  "image_name": "63b7b0e6d676b800f02b2b1750660552.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several electrochemical reactor concepts that use in situ hydrogen peroxide and related oxidation chemistry to convert simple feedstocks into more valuable products. Across the panels, membrane-separated cells bring oxygen, water, and small molecules such as ethylene or methane into contact with cathodes, anodes, catalysts, and acidic layers. A recurring idea is that oxygen is reduced at the cathode to H2O2, while water oxidation occurs at the anode, and the generated peroxide is either collected or immediately consumed in chemical upgrading steps.\n\nOne scheme couples peroxide formation to ethylene conversion, showing peroxide production from oxygen and water and then reaction of ethylene over a catalyst/solid acid bed to form ethylene glycol, with gas recycling loops. Another set of diagrams focuses on a membrane-electrode structure where peroxide concentration is highest near the cathode and decreases across the solid electrolyte layer, enabling reaction with incoming C2H4. Additional panels depict methane oxidation pathways, where electrochemical peroxide generation and chemical steps lead toward oxygenated products such as formic acid or methanol-like intermediates. The final panels highlight an electro-Fenton strategy, using Fe2+/Fe3+ and hydroxyl radicals under pressure or in a linear reactor, to upgrade light alkanes into value-added products.",
